@violation-signature Seats spend days on a single-consumer closure while a shared piece that would close four problems waits; a ranking from last week is still driving dispatch after the statement set changed.
! conclusion: Rank shared pieces by how many consumers each one closes, alone or with the pieces ranked above it; dispatch widest first; recompute the ranking whenever the set of statements changes.

  + context: There are more units than seats, and some units are shared foundations that several consumers wait on.

  + IF:
    Dispatch goes to the nearest closure, the most familiar problem, or the order the rows were written in.

  + HOWEVER:
    Single-consumer closures each unlock one thing. A shared foundation unlocks every consumer that was waiting on it, and its consumers' remaining work becomes assembly.

  + THEN:
    Count, for each shared piece, the problems whose only remaining open parts it closes. Order by that count. Put the units that sit before every other unit's consumer first. When statements are repaired, added or withdrawn, recount: the ranking is a function of the set.

  + BECAUSE:
    Throughput is consumers closed per seat-hour, and a shared piece's count is exactly that number for its seat.

    + evidence: Lean: e0d0d5a74 (FOUNDATION-RANKING.md, 2026-10-13) ranked Sard at four consumers after the statement repairs that day had raised it from one; t96J05 was then closed in 263a434fc (2026-10-18). The same file records the recount: before the repairs, projective rational homology was first.
    + evidence: WM: fad572386 dispatch recommendation puts P00 to P03 first because they are the consumers every other packet's output passes through; P00 moved to the head when it was added, without renumbering the rest.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Write the ranking as a table with a count column and a date; re-derive it after any statement repair.
    - When the seats are idle and the top-ranked piece is not out, that is the dispatch.
